/* ============================================================================
matrix-power.epher -- A^5 and A^0, and Fibonacci numbers from A^n

The built-in ^ raises a square matrix to a power, A^0 being the identity.
With A = [[1,1],[1,0]] the powers carry the Fibonacci numbers:
A^n = [[F(n+1), F(n)], [F(n), F(n-1)]] where F_0 = 0, F_1 = 1 (the
identity is proved by induction; see any text on Fibonacci numbers, e.g.
Vorobiev, "Fibonacci Numbers"). So A^12 = [[233, 144], [144, 89]] and its
entries are F13 = 233, F12 = 144 and F11 = 89. All values are exact.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/algebra/matrices/matrix-power.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\algebra\matrices\matrix-power.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/algebra/matrices/matrix-power.epher

The knob to edit is the matrix A below.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ----------------------------------------------------------------
const A = [[1, 1], [1, 0]]

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
print("A^0 (the identity):", A ^ 0)
print("A^2:", A ^ 2)
print("A^5:", A ^ 5)
P12 = A ^ 12                              // = [[F13, F12], [F12, F11]] (echoes)
print("F12:", P12[1][2])
print("F13:", P12[1][1])
print("F11:", P12[2][2])
/* ---- expected output ----
= [[1, 1], [1, 0]]
= A^0 (the identity): [[1, 0], [0, 1]]
= A^2: [[2, 1], [1, 1]]
= A^5: [[8, 5], [5, 3]]
= [[233, 144], [144, 89]]
= F12: 144
= F13: 233
= F11: 89
*/
